{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's what happens to sectional gutters near Lake Guntersville:</p>

<p>Standard sectional gutters come in 10-foot sections joined with slip connectors and sealant. Four sections to cover 40 feet, which means three seams that have to stay watertight. In a normal environment, that sealant lasts 8-12 years before it needs attention.</p>

<p>Near the lake? Cut that in half. The humidity at <a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond</a> and along <a href="/recent-projects/">Tupelo Pike</a> hovers at 80-90% on summer mornings. That moisture gets into seams and stays there. The sealant cycles between wet and dry constantly, breaking down the bond. By year 5-6, you've got seams that weep. By year 8-10, they're actively leaking onto your fascia.</p>

<p>Vacation homes have it worse. A full-time resident might notice water staining on the fascia and call someone. A vacation home can leak onto the same spot for six months before anyone sees it. The fascia rots. The rot spreads to soffit and potentially to rafter tails. What started as a failed gutter seam becomes a $2,000-3,000 repair.</p>

<p>The older homes in <a href="/recent-projects/">downtown Scottsboro</a> face a different version of this problem. Original gutters from the 60s and 70s have seams that have been re-caulked multiple times. Each repair works for a while. Eventually you're caulking over caulk over caulk, and nothing adheres properly anymore.</p>

<p>Seamless gutters solve this by eliminating the problem. No seams along the runs means no seams to fail. The only joints are at corners and downspouts — and those are welded or sealed in ways that hold up far better than section-to-section connections.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are fabricated from continuous aluminum coil stock — one piece, custom-cut to the exact length of each run on your home. Instead of 10-foot sections joined together, your 47'8" gutter is actually 47'8" of material with no internal connections.</p>

<p>We bring a portable gutter machine to your property. It's basically a rolling mill on a trailer. Flat aluminum coil goes in one end, formed gutter profile comes out the other. We measure to the quarter-inch, dial in the length, and run it. What comes out is ready to install.</p>

<p>The material is .027 gauge aluminum standard, or .032 gauge for lake properties and situations with higher corrosion risk. That doesn't sound like much difference, but .032 is about 18% thicker — noticeably more rigid and more resistant to denting and corrosion over time.</p>

<p>Quick truth about "seamless": there are still seams. Just not along the runs. You've got joints at inside corners, outside corners, and where downspout outlets connect. On a simple rectangular house, that might be 8-10 joints total instead of 20-25 with sectional. Fewer joints means fewer failure points, which means longer life and less maintenance.</p>
{% endset %}

